from common import lesson, lab, cite, run, code

LESSONS = [
lesson(0,0,'orientation','What this course makes you able to do',
 'Enough Python and PyTorch to load a small model, read its activations by shape, and test what you built, written for someone who already programs for a living.',
 '''<h2 id="the-job">The job is to be fluent, not clever</h2>
<p>Every interpretability toolkit is Python: the model loaders, the hook APIs, the notebooks that papers ship, the eval harnesses. If you have worked with Elixir and Rust code, even mostly by generating and reading it, Python's concepts will be familiar. What slows people down is different: Python has no compiler to tell you a shape is wrong, so a <code>(batch, seq, d_model)</code> tensor that silently became <code>(seq, d_model)</code> produces a plausible number instead of an error. Syntax takes weeks. Being able to say, before you run a line, what shape comes out of it takes months, and that is the skill this course is built around.</p>
<p>The target is concrete. At the end you will load GPT-2 small, capture the residual stream after every one of its twelve blocks with forward hooks, assert that every captured tensor is <code>(batch, seq, 768)</code>, save the activations with a manifest someone else can check, and have the eval-pipeline labs green. That is the readiness test the <a href="../../../ROADMAP.md">roadmap</a> names for the first barrier, and it is what the <a href="16-readiness-review.html">readiness review</a> asks of you.</p>
<div class="diagram">Python habits → arrays and shapes → tensors and autograd → modules and hooks
                                                → activations you can test and save</div>
<h2 id="path">Four modules and a review</h2>
<p><strong>Module 1</strong> teaches Python by contrast with the Elixir and Rust you have seen. Names bind to objects and mutation is visible through every alias, which is the opposite of the BEAM's immutability and of Rust's ownership. Exceptions are the error channel, not <code>{:ok, _}</code> or <code>Result</code>. The practice is the three eval-pipeline functions that already ship with tests: <code>score_ticket</code>, <code>load_cases</code>, <code>summarize_run</code>. You will write them spec-first, which is the habit you already have.</p>
<p><strong>Module 2</strong> is the spine. NumPy arrays and their shapes, broadcasting as a rule you can apply by hand, reductions over named axes, einsum strings read aloud, then the same ideas in torch with dtypes, devices, views, in-place operations, and autograd. There is a shape-prediction drill and a lab whose tests are shape assertions.</p>
<p><strong>Module 3</strong> treats models as objects. <code>nn.Module</code> trees, parameters and <code>state_dict</code>, GPT-2 small through <code>transformers</code>, forward hooks, and then the same activation captured three ways: a raw hook, TransformerLens 4, and nnsight. You will see which numbers agree, which differ, and why. The module ends with seeds, safetensors, a run manifest, and what fits in CPU memory.</p>
<p><strong>The review</strong> is a rubric, not a quiz. It lists the abilities and which lesson to revisit if one is missing.</p>
<h2 id="abilities">Three professional abilities</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Read:</strong> open a notebook from a paper and know, line by line, what shape each tensor has and whether a name is an alias or a copy.</li>
<li><strong>Build:</strong> write a function from its test, capture an activation with a hook, and remove the hook afterwards even when the forward pass raises.</li>
<li><strong>Check:</strong> assert every shape, keep autograd out of inference code, and leave a manifest that lets a colleague verify the file you produced.</li></ul>
<h2 id="how-to-read">How to read the lessons</h2>
<p>Each lesson has the same parts: a mechanism shown in a small script, a terminal block with the real output of that script, a pitfall, a judgment question with a revealable explanation, and practice with an answer. The terminal blocks are not illustrations. Every command was executed by <code>scripts/verify_commands.py</code> and the output next to it is what came back, with versions recorded in <a href="../research/verify/lab-transcript.txt">the transcript</a>. If your output differs, the difference is information: a version, a platform, or a mistake, and the lesson on reproducibility will tell you which.</p>
<p>Predict before you reveal. When a lesson prints a shape, cover it and say the shape first. When it shows an error, say which line raises. The miss is the lesson.</p>
<p>The scripts live in <code>labs/examples/</code> and are short enough to read in full. Run them from the course directory, <code>tutorials/python/python-tut</code>, after the setup in <a href="01-lab-setup.html">lesson 01</a>. Here is the one you will run first:</p>
'''+run('python3 labs/examples/check_setup.py')+'''
<p>Three numbers there will follow you through the course: a batch of 2, a sequence of 5 tokens, and 768 features per token, which is GPT-2 small's <code>d_model</code>. You will see <code>(2, 5, 768)</code> so often that reading it as "two prompts, five positions, one vector each" should become automatic.</p>
<h2 id="siblings">Where this sits</h2>
<p>The evals side of your goal is covered by <a href="../../../evals/evals-tut/index.html">Understanding LLM Evals</a> and the rigor by <a href="../../../statistics/statistics-tut/index.html">Statistics for Evals</a>. This course feeds <a href="../../../linear-algebra/linear-algebra-tut/index.html">Linear Algebra for Interpretability</a>, which projects real activations onto directions, and <a href="../../../transformers/transformers-tut/index.html">Transformer Anatomy</a>, which rebuilds the GPT-2 forward pass from the weights you will learn to read here. <a href="../../../interpretability/interpretability-tut/index.html">Interpretability Methods and Rigor</a> depends on all of them.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p>Most bugs that cost a week are not algorithmic. They are a hook left registered, a tensor that still carries a gradient graph into a cache, a batch dimension dropped by an index, or a padded position averaged into a mean. All four are shape and object-identity mistakes, and all four are caught by the habits this course drills.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Treating Python as "Rust without types" and skipping the fundamentals because the syntax looks easy. The semantics of names, mutation and views are where the surprises are, and they bite hardest inside a 200-line notebook you did not write.</p></div>''',
 'A colleague says their activation cache is "about 100 MB" and the saved file is 2 GB. Before reading any code, what are your first two hypotheses?',
 'Either the cached tensors still carry autograd graphs and were saved with them, or every batch was captured with a hook that was never removed, so later forward passes appended more. Both are object-lifetime mistakes, not arithmetic ones. Lesson 13 shows the second directly and lesson 15 sizes the first.',
 lab('Read <code>labs/examples/check_setup.py</code> top to bottom. For each <code>print</code>, say what will appear before running it. Then run it.',
 'The point is not the versions. It is that <code>x.shape</code> is a tuple you can index, that <code>x.shape[-1]</code> is the feature dimension by convention, and that you can predict the output of a script you have read. Keep that habit for every script in the course.',
 ['python3 labs/examples/check_setup.py']), ['py-datamodel'], 'overview goals shapes readiness'),

lesson(1,0,'lab-setup','Lab setup: a CPU-only toolchain you can trust',
 'A virtualenv, a CPU build of torch, pinned versions you can report, GPT-2 small in the local cache, and the habit of checking shapes interactively.',
 '''<h2 id="why-local">Why a local, CPU-only setup is enough</h2>
<p>GPT-2 small has 124 million parameters and runs a five-token prompt on a laptop CPU in well under a second. Everything in this course, and most of the next three, fits in a few gigabytes of RAM with no GPU. A free Colab notebook works too and is covered below, but a local virtualenv is worth having because it is the environment you will run tests in, and tests are how this course checks you.</p>
<p>The authoring environment is recorded at the top of <a href="../research/verify/lab-transcript.txt">the transcript</a>: Python 3.13, torch 2.14.1 CPU build, transformers 5.20.2, transformer_lens 4.2.0, nnsight 0.8.1, numpy 2.5.4. You do not need those exact versions, but when an output differs from the lesson, compare versions first.</p>
<h2 id="venv">Create the environment</h2>
<p>From the course directory. These are instructions, not a transcript: the verifier does not run installers, and the only outputs shown on this page are from the two commands at the bottom.</p>
'''+code('''python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate            # Windows: .venv\\Scripts\\activate
python3 -m pip install --upgrade pip
python3 -m pip install numpy
python3 -m pip install torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
python3 -m pip install transformers safetensors
python3 -m pip install transformer-lens nnsight   # for lesson 14; optional until then
python3 -m pip freeze > research/my-versions.txt   # what you actually got''', 'sh')+'''
<p>Three things deserve a sentence each. The <code>--index-url</code> for torch selects the CPU wheel; the default PyPI wheel on Linux pulls in several gigabytes of CUDA libraries you cannot use without a GPU. If you install <code>torchvision</code> later for the interpretability course, install it from the same CPU index in the same command, because a torchvision built for CUDA will fail to import against a CPU torch. And <code>pip freeze</code> is the cheapest reproducibility habit there is: a file you can paste into a bug report.</p>
<p>The <code>.venv</code> directory is ignored by git in this course. Never commit it, and never install into a virtualenv someone else's tests depend on without asking.</p>
<h2 id="colab">The Colab alternative</h2>
<p>A free Colab runtime already has torch and transformers installed. Upload or clone this repository, open a notebook, and run <code>!pip install transformer-lens nnsight</code> in the first cell. Two differences matter. Colab's torch is usually a CUDA build, so <code>torch.cuda.is_available()</code> may be true and tensors created on the GPU must be moved with <code>.cpu()</code> before <code>.numpy()</code>. And a notebook cell is a file for nnsight's purposes, so its <code>trace</code> blocks work there, while pasting one into an interactive <code>python3</code> prompt does not (lesson 14 explains why).</p>
<h2 id="gpt2">Get GPT-2 small into the cache once</h2>
<p>The first time <code>AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained("gpt2")</code> runs it downloads about 550 MB into <code>~/.cache/huggingface/hub</code>. After that, set <code>HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1</code> in your shell so that later runs never touch the network, which makes them faster, deterministic, and runnable on a train. The example scripts set that variable themselves if you have not. If a script fails with a message about not being able to reach the Hub, run it once without the variable to download, then set it again.</p>
<h2 id="interactive">Check shapes interactively</h2>
<p>You will spend a lot of time at a prompt asking tensors what they are. Three habits make that fast. Start <code>python3 -i labs/examples/check_setup.py</code> to run a script and drop into a prompt with its variables alive. At the prompt, <code>x.shape</code>, <code>x.dtype</code> and <code>x.device</code> answer the three questions that matter; <code>type(x)</code> tells you whether you hold a tensor, a tuple of tensors, or a <code>ModelOutput</code>. And a one-line assert, <code>assert x.shape == (2, 5, 768), x.shape</code>, is cheaper than any amount of printing: it fails loudly, and the message tells you what you had.</p>
<p>Two shell variables are worth setting while you learn: <code>HF_HUB_DISABLE_PROGRESS_BARS=1</code> keeps progress bars out of transcripts, and <code>TRANSFORMERS_VERBOSITY=error</code> hides advisory warnings so a real one stands out.</p>
<h2 id="verify">Verify the setup</h2>
<p>Run the check script, then the environment tests. The test file ships passing; it tests your machine, not you. If torch is missing the torch tests skip with a message that says so instead of failing, which is the behaviour every lab test in this course has.</p>
'''+run('python3 labs/examples/check_setup.py')+run("python3 -m unittest discover -s labs -p 'test_environment.py' -v")+'''
<p>Read the names of the tests as a preview: thirteen hidden states of <code>(batch, seq, 768)</code>, logits of <code>(1, 5, 50257)</code>, and a config of 12 layers, 768 features and 12 heads. Those are the numbers of GPT-2 small, and lesson 12 explains each.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p>A result you cannot reproduce on a second machine is a result you cannot defend. The habit starts here: one environment file, offline runs by default, and a test you can run on arrival that says whether the machine is ready.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Installing torch from PyPI on a Linux box without a GPU, waiting for several gigabytes, and then installing torchvision from a different index. The CPU index exists to avoid both problems; use it for every torch-family package at once.</p></div>''',
 'Your laptop and a colleague\'s produce slightly different hidden-state values for the same prompt, around 1e-6 apart. Is the setup broken?',
 'Probably not. Floating-point results are not guaranteed identical across platforms, BLAS libraries, or torch versions; the reproducibility notes say so explicitly. A difference of 1e-6 in float32 with identical argmax is normal. A difference of 1e-1, or a different top token, is a real bug: different weights, different tokenization, or a hook changing something.',
 lab('Run the environment tests with <code>-v</code> and then deliberately break something: unset <code>HF_HUB_OFFLINE</code> and rename your Hugging Face cache directory temporarily, and run again. Read how the GPT-2 tests report it. Restore the cache afterwards.',
 'The GPT-2 tests should skip with a message naming the cache, not error. A test suite that distinguishes "cannot run here" from "wrong" is one you can trust in a CI log. Notice also that the NumPy and torch tests still ran: the skip is per class.',
 ["python3 -m unittest discover -s labs -p 'test_environment.py' -v"]), ['torch-random','hf-gpt2'], 'install virtualenv pip torch cpu colab cache offline'),
]
